1. Flickering Torch Glow (1 Keyframe + Graph Editor Noise Modifier)

Instead of keyframing every frame by hand, Blender has a procedural Noise curve modifier made for fire flicker:

Select the Flames: Left-click Brazier_Flame_Glare_Meshes in the 3D Viewport.
Open the Shader Editor: Switch to the Shading workspace (remember to click the Viewport Shading sphere + Compositor: Always if needed) or split a panel into a Shader Editor.
Insert 1 Keyframe on Emission:
Find the Emission node (its Strength is 7.2).
Hover your mouse directly over the Strength number box and press I (or Right-Click → Insert Keyframe). The box will turn yellow.
Important Tip: Keep that Emission node clicked/highlighted (white outline) so Blender shows its curve in the Graph Editor!
Add the Flicker (Graph Editor):
Change your bottom Timeline panel (top-left editor icon) to the Graph Editor (if you don't see Strength on the left list, click the little arrow/cursor icon "Only Show Selected" at the top header to turn that filter off).
Click Strength in the left channel list, then press N on your keyboard to open the right sidebar and click the Modifiers tab.
Click Add Modifier → Noise.
Tune the Fire Flicker:
Scale: Set to 3.5 – 5.0 (controls flicker speed; larger = smoother).
Strength: Set to 3.5 – 4.5 (controls how much brighter/dimmer the glow jumps).
Press Spacebar—all 10 torches and their Bloom glare will flicker continuously!
Bonus: Select any Brazier_Warm_Fill point light, go to Object Data Properties (green lightbulb icon), press I on Power, and add the same Noise modifier so the orange light on the nearby walls flickers too.
2. Physically Dancing 3D Flames (Wave Modifier — No Keyframes Needed!)

To make the actual 3D flame cones and floating sparks wiggle like real fire:

Select Brazier_Flame_Glare_Meshes in the 3D Viewport.
Go to Modifier Properties (the blue wrench icon on the right panel).
Click Add Modifier → Deform → Wave (or type Wave in the search bar).
Adjust these sliders so the motion fits the small torches:
Height: 0.07 (default 0.5 is too big—0.07 gives a nice subtle flame lick)
Width: 0.45
Narrowness: 1.8
Expand Time → set Speed to 0.20
Press Spacebar—the Wave modifier animates automatically with the timeline!
3. Slow "Breathing" Pulse on the Glowing Maze Cracks & Crystals

For magical cracks and abyss crystals, a slow breathing pulse looks great alongside the fast-flickering torches:

Select the Maze (or Crystals): Click Maze_Platform in the 3D Viewport (or click Abyss_Crystal_Glare_Meshes and choose one of its color slots in the Material Properties red-sphere tab).
Keyframe Low → High → Low in the Shader Editor:
Frame 1: Move the blue playhead in the Timeline to 1. On the Emission node, set Strength to 2.5, hover over it, and press I.
Frame 45: Drag the playhead to 45. Change Strength to 8.5, hover over it, and press I.
Frame 90: Drag the playhead to 90. Change Strength back to 2.5, hover over it, and press I.
Make It Loop Forever:
Keep the Emission node selected, move your mouse into the Timeline (or Graph Editor), press A to select the yellow keyframes, and press Shift + E → Make Cyclic (F-Modifier).
Press Spacebar—your multi-colored stone cracks will smoothly pulse bright and dim forever!
4. Optional Bonus: Floating START & END Signs
Click START_Sign_Glare_Mesh and open Object Properties (orange square icon on the right).
At Frame 1, hover over Location Z (4.15 m) and press I.
Go to Frame 50, type 4.45 into Location Z, and press I.
Go to Frame 100, type 4.15 back into Location Z, and press I.
Hover over the Timeline, press A, then Shift + E → Make Cyclic (F-Modifier) so the sign gently floats up and down forever.
